<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<component type="desktop-application">
  <id>dev.cominotti.lushtext</id>

  <name>LushText</name>
  <summary>A minimalist text editor with workspace support</summary>

  <developer id="dev.cominotti">
    <name>Danilo Cominotti</name>
  </developer>

  <metadata_license>CC0-1.0</metadata_license>
  <project_license>GPL-3.0-or-later</project_license>

  <description>
    <p>
      LushText is a fast, minimalist text editor for GNOME. It combines a
      focused GtkSourceView editing surface with persistent workspaces,
      adaptive document properties, Markdown preview, notes, local history,
      and careful draft recovery.
    </p>
    <p>Features:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Persistent workspace sidebar with ordered workspace folders</li>
      <li>Adaptive document properties pane for metadata, file health, and formatting details</li>
      <li>Focus Mode for distraction-free writing</li>
      <li>Native Markdown preview with tables, task lists, callouts, footnotes, and local images</li>
      <li>Syntax highlighting via GtkSourceView</li>
      <li>EditorConfig-aware indentation settings</li>
      <li>Bookmarks, document notes, and folder notes</li>
      <li>Local history snapshots for saved files</li>
      <li>Workspace search and safe multi-file Replace All with undo</li>
      <li>Session restore, autosaved drafts, and external file change monitoring</li>
      <li>Large-file safeguards, minimap, dark mode, and customizable editor font</li>
    </ul>
  </description>

  <launchable type="desktop-id">dev.cominotti.lushtext.desktop</launchable>

  <url type="homepage">https://github.com/cominotti/lushtext</url>
  <url type="bugtracker">https://github.com/cominotti/lushtext/issues</url>
  <url type="vcs-browser">https://github.com/cominotti/lushtext</url>

  <branding>
    <color type="primary" scheme_preference="light">#d946ef</color>
    <color type="primary" scheme_preference="dark">#8b5cf6</color>
  </branding>

  <content_rating type="oars-1.1"/>

  <supports>
    <control>pointing</control>
    <control>keyboard</control>
  </supports>

  <requires>
    <display_length compare="ge">360</display_length>
  </requires>

  <screenshots>
    <screenshot type="default">
      <caption>Editing a text file with the workspace sidebar</caption>
      <image>https://raw.githubusercontent.com/cominotti/lushtext/main/data/screenshots/editor.png</image>
    </screenshot>
  </screenshots>

  <releases>
    <release version="0.4.0" date="2026-06-16">
      <description>
        <p>## Poetic Opening</p>
        <p>Source checked: Folger Shakespeare Library, "Sonnet 18," final couplet.</p>
        <p>Selection: Complete final couplet.</p>
        <p>Complete stanza:</p>
        <p>&gt; So long as men can breathe or eyes can see,</p>
        <p>&gt; So long lives this, and this gives life to thee.</p>
        <p>William Shakespeare, "Sonnet 18"</p>
        <p>## What's Changed</p>
        <p>LushText now has a GNOME-style Open popover for recent documents. The header Open button and Ctrl+K open a searchable recent list with file names, parent paths, age labels, row removal, empty/search states, and a direct path back to the file chooser. Recent rows are app-owned and bounded: LushText records local file paths and timestamps after explicit opens, excludes files that are already open, prunes missing or unsupported entries, and keeps startup session restore from reshuffling your recent history.</p>
        <p>The command palette can now search note records directly. Notes mode searches bookmarks, folder notes, document notes, and note-related open tabs using the same note taxonomy as Browse Notes, including previews and workspace-aware grouping. All mode now includes notes alongside files and commands, so remembered context is reachable without opening a separate notes browser first.</p>
        <p>Note editing feels calmer. Existing notes open in rendered preview mode when there is meaningful saved content, empty notes open in edit mode, and Save is available only for meaningful changes instead of whitespace-only edits.</p>
        <p>The tab strip now stays visible even when only one normal tab is open, making tab context actions easier to discover and keeping the document chrome more consistent.</p>
        <p>Preferences and supporting tools received polish too. The Background Opacity wording now more clearly describes editor and Markdown preview document backgrounds, the Data page wording is tidier, and the development surface gained focused command-palette-notes smoke/manual fixtures plus stronger visual proof coverage for the new Open popover.</p>
        <p>The Snap packaging work is now captured in OpenSpec and maintainer documentation as preparation work. This release does not add a new public Snap channel.</p>
        <p>## Manual Actions Needed</p>
        <p>No manual action is needed for normal users upgrading from v0.3.0.</p>
        <p>Maintainers should review the release workflow output after publication: the Cominotti Flatpak repository remains the primary Flatpak channel, while any Flathub handoff is optional and depends on the configured GitHub Actions secrets. Snap packaging remains preparation-only until its external store and platform gates are ready.</p>
        <p>## Warnings and Deprecations</p>
        <p>LushText now writes an app-owned `recent-documents.json` file for the Open popover. It stores local paths, canonical identities when available, and last-opened timestamps, but not document text, note bodies, drafts, local-history contents, or private sidecar identifiers.</p>
        <p>Recent Open currently supports local filesystem documents only. Unsupported URI-style entries are ignored or pruned instead of being offered as reopen targets.</p>
        <p>Malformed or unreadable note, bookmark, or sidecar metadata may be skipped from note search while LushText records recovery diagnostics. This keeps search safe and bounded, but it means damaged metadata may not appear in the command palette until the underlying sidecar is recoverable.</p>
        <p>No deprecations are introduced in this release.</p>
        <p>## Bug Fixes</p>
        <p>Recent Open now refreshes from the real open-tab set, so stale open-path cache entries no longer hide valid recent documents after tabs close, rename, save-as, or external-file transitions.</p>
        <p>The Open popover rows better match the intended GNOME-style shape: long paths use tooltips and ellipsizing instead of widening the popover, the list avoids horizontal scrolling, and search/reset behavior is steadier across repeated openings.</p>
        <p>Command palette note refreshes are non-destructive, so loading or updating note rows no longer clears the active palette query or disrupts the visible search state.</p>
        <p>Folder-note wording in AppStream metadata now matches the current user-facing model, and the Background Opacity preference text is clearer about what becomes transparent.</p>
      </description>
    </release>
    <release version="0.3.0" date="2026-06-14">
      <description>
        <p>## Poetic Opening</p>
        <p>Source checked: Wikisource, "The Poems of Oscar Wilde/Symphony in Yellow" from The Poems of Oscar Wilde (1909), first printed stanza.</p>
        <p>Selection: Complete first stanza.</p>
        <p>Complete stanza:</p>
        <p>&gt; An omnibus across the bridge</p>
        <p>&gt; Crawls like a yellow butterfly,</p>
        <p>&gt; And, here and there, a passer-by</p>
        <p>&gt; Shows like a little restless midge.</p>
        <p>Oscar Wilde, "Symphony in Yellow"</p>
        <p>## What's Changed</p>
        <p>LushText workspaces are now named, ordered folder sets instead of one-folder roots. You can create an empty workspace by name, add one or more folders afterward, reorder top-level folders without moving anything on disk, collapse busy workspace sections, and keep overlapping folders visible in the sidebar while search, the command palette, notes, and bookmarks de-duplicate results by file identity.</p>
        <p>The app-data safety story is much stronger. LushText now has a startup format preflight, a `Preferences &gt; Data` status page, backup-before-replacement behavior for future format upgrades or Start Fresh decisions, recovery quarantine for damaged metadata, retryable migration ledgers after in-app renames, and clearer diagnostics when session, draft, sidecar, local-history, search, or Replace All evidence cannot be fully trusted.</p>
        <p>Saving and Replace All now share more of the same durable filesystem contract. Writes coordinate by stable target, preserve symlink-backed saves by updating the resolved file, distinguish ordinary write failures from durability-unconfirmed writes, and keep Replace All undo evidence in per-file durable journals with safety caps for large or actively changing files.</p>
        <p>The workspace sidebar is easier to read while you work. Sticky click selection no longer pretends that a row is special, open files and the active tab now have explicit row indicators, reorder handles refresh after sidebar mutations, zero-folder workspaces have clearer empty states, and the workspace sidebar plus document properties pane now share a calmer side-rail style.</p>
        <p>Several everyday editor surfaces received polish: the bottom status bar is more legible without becoming a second toolbar, the minimap behaves more steadily during sidebar animation and dense viewport changes, inline alerts sit more naturally in the editor, and Markdown preview now handles reference-style and markdown-it-style inline footnotes more faithfully.</p>
        <p>Maintainers and automation users get a larger proof surface. LushText now exposes a documented read-only D-Bus automation snapshot, a machine-readable action catalog, reusable automation client commands, visual/crash/portal/accessibility/performance smoke lanes, GTK Lush proof tooling, Blueprint template checks, mutation testing, and release-lane fixes for Flatpak and Flathub handoff workflows.</p>
        <p>## Manual Actions Needed</p>
        <p>No manual action is needed for normal users upgrading from v0.2.0.</p>
        <p>If you are building LushText from source, update to Rust 1.96.0 or newer and install `blueprint-compiler` before regenerating UI templates. Maintainers should still confirm whether the release workflow deploys the Cominotti Flatpak repository directly or uploads the `cominotti-flatpak-repository` artifact for manual publication, and should review any optional Flathub pull request before merging it.</p>
        <p>## Warnings and Deprecations</p>
        <p>Folder notes now use consistent folder-note terminology. Existing legacy sidecars remain recognized as compatibility data, but new user-facing labels and current storage use current folder-note terms.</p>
        <p>Public-era v1 app metadata is expected to continue normally. Older pre-public or unsupported bare JSON metadata is not silently treated as current data; LushText preserves it through recovery or format-upgrade backup paths before starting fresh or writing a current replacement.</p>
        <p>Future-version app metadata written by a newer LushText cannot be downgraded by this release. If that state is detected at startup, the safest choice is to quit and reopen it with the newer app; Start Fresh is available only after preservation succeeds.</p>
        <p>The minimap and Replace All safety limits remain intentionally conservative for very large or rapidly changing files. LushText may hide expensive minimap details, skip files that changed after search, or refuse undo payloads that exceed configured safety bounds to protect responsiveness and user data.</p>
        <p>Snap packaging remains preparation-only until its external store and GNOME platform gates are ready. The Flatpak continues to use full filesystem access for local workspace editing and does not move to portals-only file access in this release.</p>
        <p>## Bug Fixes</p>
        <p>Workspace folder reorder handles now resync after folder add, remove, reorder, refresh, and row-recycling transitions, so drag affordances do not go stale while the sidebar changes around them.</p>
        <p>Minimap viewport diagnostics, marker placement, overscroll handling, and sidebar animation interactions are steadier, reducing visual drift and layout surprises in dense documents and constrained windows.</p>
        <p>Replace All undo recovery is more robust after partial failures, and modified or actively saving tabs are skipped more consistently instead of being overwritten by a broad replace operation.</p>
        <p>Recovery metadata handling now preserves malformed, oversized, unreadable, unsupported, or unsafe app-owned state before defaults or repairs are written, reducing the chance that a startup recovery problem becomes silent data loss.</p>
        <p>The release lane includes fixes for the Cominotti Flatpak workflow, Flathub runtime remote setup, trusted workspace handling in container jobs, and recovery-ref reruns when publication tooling needs to be repaired after a public tag exists.</p>
      </description>
    </release>
    <release version="0.2.0" date="2026-06-01">
      <description>
        <p>## Poetic Opening</p>
        <p>Source checked: Folger Shakespeare Library, "Shakespeare's Sonnets - Sonnet 116", final rhyming couplet.</p>
        <p>Selection: Complete final couplet.</p>
        <p>Complete couplet:</p>
        <p>&gt; If this be error, and upon me proved,</p>
        <p>&gt; I never writ, nor no man ever loved.</p>
        <p>William Shakespeare, "Sonnet 116"</p>
        <p>## What's Changed</p>
        <p>LushText now has a first-party Cominotti Flatpak publication path. The release workflow can generate signed repository metadata for the Cominotti Flatpak remote, publish the shared `cominotti.flatpakrepo` remote descriptor, and provide a LushText-specific `lushtext.flatpakref` installer while keeping Flathub as a reviewable secondary handoff.</p>
        <p>The editor minimap is more faithful to the document you are actually seeing. Search, bookmark, modified-line, and long-line markers now project through the rendered GtkSourceMap geometry, so they stay aligned with wrapped text and dynamic end-of-file overscroll instead of drifting into the blank tail below the document.</p>
        <p>Adaptive editor geometry is steadier in tight windows. The status bar and its quick document controls stay visible, the workspace sidebar and document properties settle into pane or sheet presentations without oscillating, and passive window narrowing no longer leaves the editor's left edge hidden behind the workspace surface.</p>
        <p>Changing editor transparency and theme settings is smoother across multiple tabs. LushText now deduplicates generated opacity-aware GtkSourceView style schemes and writes them off the main thread, keeping preference changes from competing with editing responsiveness.</p>
        <p>Maintainers also get a stronger release lane: Cominotti Flatpak verification, Cloudflare Pages limit checks, optional deploy hooks, Flathub source-commit correctness, release dry-run coverage, and headless Mutter debugging helpers for reproducing GTK layout issues.</p>
        <p>## Manual Actions Needed</p>
        <p>No manual action is needed for normal users upgrading from v0.1.0.</p>
        <p>Maintainers should verify that the release workflow either deploys the Cominotti Flatpak repository or uploads the `cominotti-flatpak-repository` artifact for manual publication. Optional Flathub publication still requires review of the generated pull request or artifact; automerge remains intentionally disabled.</p>
        <p>## Warnings and Deprecations</p>
        <p>There are no deprecations in this release.</p>
        <p>Normal editor windows now keep a slightly taller minimum height so the header, tab strip, editor, and status bar remain usable instead of clipping persistent controls.</p>
        <p>The minimap may hide itself for very large wrapped documents with extremely long lines. This is intentional: it protects responsiveness when a narrow minimap would otherwise need to build an enormous wrapped visual-line map.</p>
        <p>Cominotti Flatpak deployment depends on the configured signing material and deployment credentials. If those secrets or variables are absent, the release workflow reports the skipped publication step instead of claiming completion.</p>
        <p>## Bug Fixes</p>
        <p>Minimap markers now stay inside the rendered document content area and no longer stretch across blank end-of-file overscroll space.</p>
        <p>The workspace sidebar, document-properties pane, compact bottom sheet, search results, and status bar now share constrained window space more predictably, reducing GTK allocation warnings and preventing important controls from disappearing at small sizes.</p>
        <p>Window resize transitions now preserve the editor's left and top edges when the user was already at the start of the document, avoiding accidental-looking horizontal or vertical scroll jumps caused by layout changes.</p>
      </description>
    </release>
    <release version="0.1.0" date="2026-05-29">
      <description>
        <p>## Poetic Opening</p>
        <p>Source checked: Folger Shakespeare Library, "Shakespeare's Sonnets - Sonnet 18", final rhyming couplet.</p>
        <p>Selection: Complete final couplet.</p>
        <p>Complete couplet:</p>
        <p>&gt; So long as men can breathe, or eyes can see,</p>
        <p>&gt; So long lives this, and this gives life to thee.</p>
        <p>William Shakespeare, "Sonnet 18"</p>
        <p>## What's Changed</p>
        <p>LushText's first public release is here: a fast, native GNOME text editor built with GTK4, Libadwaita, GtkSourceView, and a workspace-first shape. You can open files directly, keep multiple tabs restored across launches, pin useful tabs, save and print documents, and tune the editor with dark-mode-aware syntax schemes, custom fonts, line numbers, wrapping, zoom, and GSettings-backed preferences.</p>
        <p>Workspace support is the heart of this release. You can add named workspace folders, switch between a single workspace and `All workspaces`, browse files in the persistent sidebar, inspect files with quick peek, drill into deep folders with Focus Folder, and let visible folders refresh as the filesystem changes.</p>
        <p>Editing is paired with recovery and safety work. LushText autosaves drafts, restores sessions, detects external file changes, uses durable atomic saves, offers explicit save/discard close flows, preserves line-ending and encoding state, and degrades intentionally on very large files instead of freezing or risking data loss.</p>
        <p>Search and navigation cover both the current tab and the current workspace scope. The command palette searches files and commands, per-tab find/replace handles local edits, and workspace search supports streaming results, regex or literal modes, whole-word matching, glob filters, search history, saved searches, match navigation, and a previewable multi-file Replace All flow with undo.</p>
        <p>Markdown files get a native preview alongside the source editor or as a focused full-width view. The renderer covers headings, emphasis, code, links, ordered and unordered lists, task lists, nested blockquotes, GitHub alert callouts, footnotes, definition lists, tables, and local images with clear fallback states.</p>
        <p>LushText also ships line bookmarks, document notes, folder notes, a unified notes browser, local history snapshots with preview and restore undo, a minimap with useful document markers, adaptive document properties, and Focus Mode for a quieter writing surface.</p>
        <p>## Manual Actions Needed</p>
        <p>No manual action is needed for this first release.</p>
        <p>Maintainers publishing through Flathub should still review the generated Flathub pull request before merging. The workflow intentionally does not enable Flathub automerge.</p>
        <p>## Warnings and Deprecations</p>
        <p>There are no upgrade deprecations because this is the first public GitHub release.</p>
        <p>Large files intentionally lose some editor features as they grow: files above 10 MB disable syntax highlighting, files above 50 MB disable undo and local-history browsing, and files above 500 MB are refused.</p>
        <p>The Flatpak uses broad host filesystem access because workspace editing, search, rename, delete, file monitoring, notes, and local history all need normal local-file behavior. LushText does not request network access.</p>
        <p>Bookmark, note, and local-history sidecars follow saved file paths and in-app renames, but they do not automatically follow external moves or copies performed outside LushText.</p>
        <p>EditorConfig support currently covers indentation settings only. Line endings, charset, trailing-whitespace trimming, final-newline insertion, and max-line-length handling are not applied yet.</p>
        <p>Snap packaging is scaffolded, but it is not ready for users until the GNOME 50 Snap platform is available.</p>
        <p>## Bug Fixes</p>
        <p>This first release includes the reliability fixes that made the public cut: durable save ordering, draft/session recovery hardening, safer close flows, and preservation of modified state when a save fails.</p>
        <p>It also includes polish and correctness fixes across the workspace sidebar, search panel, Markdown preview layout, inline alerts, desktop identity, Flatpak packaging, and headless widget-test harness.</p>
      </description>
    </release>
  </releases>

  <translation type="gettext">lushtext</translation>

  <custom>
    <value key="GnomeSoftware::key-colors">[(217, 70, 239)]</value>
  </custom>
</component>
